# AMP Prep environment variables.
#
# Copy to .env.local for development. In production set these in the host's
# environment (Fly secrets, Railway variables, systemd unit) rather than in a
# file on the volume.
#
# Only the two REQUIRED variables below are needed to run the site. Everything
# else is optional and the app degrades cleanly without it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED in production
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signing secret for the session cookie. lib/auth.ts throws on startup in
# production if this is unset OR left at the literal development default
# "local-dev-secret-change-in-production" — the app will not serve a single
# request. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# Leave blank here on purpose: an accidentally-copied placeholder is worse than
# an empty value, because the empty value fails immediately and visibly.
JWT_SECRET=

# Absolute path to the SQLite database file. Optional: when unset the path is
# resolved relative to the working directory, so starting the process from the
# wrong place silently creates a new empty database instead of failing. Setting
# this makes the working directory irrelevant. The test suite sets it to a temp
# file (see vitest.config.ts) so tests never write to the real database.
# AMP_DB_PATH=/data/amp-prep.db

# The site's public origin, scheme included, no trailing slash.
# e.g. APP_URL=https://ampprep.example
# Read by lib/site.ts, which feeds metadataBase, og:url, the canonical link,
# every entry in sitemap.xml, and the sitemap pointer inside robots.txt. If it
# is unset the fallback is http://localhost:3000, and the live site will then
# advertise localhost URLs to crawlers and to every social share preview.
# lib/payments/index.ts also builds the post-checkout redirect from it.
APP_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email — optional, but password reset does not work without it.
#
# One variable, in nodemailer's connection form, because a half-configured
# mailer fails at send time and for a password reset that means the user waits
# for a message that never arrives:
#   SMTP_URL=smtps://user:pass@smtp.example.com:465
#
# With this unset, /forgot-password does not show a form at all. It tells the
# user to email the contact address instead, so nobody is promised a link that
# cannot be delivered. In development, unset also means reset emails are printed
# to the server log, link included, which is usually what you want locally.
SMTP_URL=
# Envelope sender. Defaults to the contact address in lib/legal.ts.
MAIL_FROM=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments — optional. Leave the whole block unset to launch free-for-everyone.
# With no provider key, /api/checkout returns 503 "Checkout is not available
# yet." and Pro is never granted. See "Launching without payments".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Selects the provider in lib/payments/index.ts. Exactly "lemonsqueezy" or
# "tap"; any other value disables checkout entirely. No provider is live, so
# leaving this and the keys below unset is the current, supported state.
PAYMENT_PROVIDER=

# Tap Payments — used when PAYMENT_PROVIDER=tap. TAP_SECRET_KEY is the switch.
TAP_SECRET_KEY=
TAP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — provisioned, but NOT yet read by any application code.
#
# The Postgres schema is applied and hardened (supabase/migrations/001-006), so
# the project is ready. No code path reads these yet: the data layer is still
# better-sqlite3, and @supabase/supabase-js is not a dependency. Setting them
# changes nothing until the port described in DEPLOYMENT.md is done. They are
# listed here so the values live in one documented place rather than being
# rediscovered later.
#
# The publishable key is designed to be public — it ships to the browser and is
# safe in version control. It is only as safe as the row level security behind
# it, which is why migration 005 exists. NEVER put the service role key in this
# file or in any NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable: it bypasses RLS entirely.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
# Server-side only. Bypasses row level security; never expose to the browser.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Content pipeline only. Not read by the running site — these are consumed by
# scripts/ during question generation, which happens on a workstation, not on
# the production host. Note that scripts/lib/env.ts reads scripts/.env only;
# it does not read this file or .env.local.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Comma-separated Gemini keys for scripts/generate-questions.ts.
# GEMINI_API_KEYS=
# Generation volume knobs (scripts/generate-questions.ts).
# AMP1_PER_DIFF=50
# AMP2_PER_DIFF=22
# Paper counts (scripts/assemble-papers.ts).
# AMP1_FREE_PAPERS=10
# AMP1_PRO_PAPERS=40
# AMP2_PRO_PAPERS=20
